draw a still game once under the menu

With the in-game menu open, every present scaled the game straight onto the
panel and then, because the menu's dim covers nearly the whole picture,
drew the same rows again into the canvas under the menu and copied them out.
The core is paused the whole time, so all of it was a picture that had not
changed. Measured on the Flip in Donkey Kong Country (Logs/slot.log of
1 November, traced, main 7843ad0): render 25.5-27.5 ms (overlay 17.8-19.6)
against a 16.7 ms period whenever the menu was up, 436 missed latches over
two visits to it -- 90% of the 485 drops in that log. Steady play on every
core drops none.

The direct pass now keys what it draws -- the frame, the grade, the effect
and the rect -- and once that has not changed for PAGES frames every page
holds it, so it is skipped, and so is recomposing an unchanged overlay over
it. An upload of the same frame no longer counts as a change. While the game
does move, rows the overlay is composed over across the game's whole width
are left out of the direct pass, since the overlay pass draws them anyway.

A menu that changes over a still game -- its cursor moving, it sliding in
-- still recomposed its whole rect, surround and game rows included, ~7 ms
of the ~20 the overlay took. What it was composed over is now kept while
the game is still and copied back instead; the game moving, a repaint or a
new rect drops it. At most a canvas of RAM, 1.7 MB, held while the game
stays still.
Estimated from the trace's stage times as ~13 ms a changing frame, not yet
measured on the Flip.

Tests draw the menu over a moving game, move its cursor over a still one,
and compare the panel with the canvas path pixel for pixel, mirrored and
not; the cursor test fails if the kept picture outlives a moving game, by
345,559 pixels. Another requires a still game under a still menu to
leave the panel untouched, and fails with the skip taken out.

Applies on top of 0042, upstream 91499a9.

